  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Support to displaced people in DRC and Rwanda via the Spanish Red Cross</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">A regional humanitarian intervention responding to the displaced people crisis linked to the conflict in eastern Democratic Republic of Congo. The project combines protection, health, mental health and psychosocial support, food security, cash assistance, and livelihood activities. It focuses especially on survivors of sexual and gender based violence, displaced households, and communities facing severe food insecurity and health risks</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Because the escalation of armed conflict in eastern DRC has led to massive displacement, collapse of basic services, high levels of food insecurity, lack of access to health care, and widespread sexual violence. The impact of the crisis extends across borders into Rwanda, where refugee camps are underfunded and unable to meet minimum needs. The project addresses urgent humanitarian gaps created by violence, displacement, and funding cuts</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project is implemented in two main locations:
&#x2022; Sud Kivu province in eastern DRC
&#x2022; Kiziba refugee camp and surrounding host communities in Rwanda
Both areas are directly affected by displacement from the conflict and by overstretched humanitarian services</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project supports displaced people, refugees, returnees, and vulnerable host communities. Priority is given to women and girls affected by sexual and gender based violence, female headed households, people with disabilities, pregnant and lactating women, and families facing acute food insecurity and limited access to health services</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Through a coordinated Red Cross Red Crescent response led by the Spanish Red Cross with the DRC Red Cross and the Rwanda Red Cross. The project delivers services via mobile clinics and humanitarian service points, provides medical and psychosocial care including PEP kits, distributes dignity kits, strengthens community based disease surveillance, and improves food security through cash transfers, agricultural training, and short cycle food production. Cash assistance is delivered using the Netherlands Red Cross 121 digital system to ensure safe, transparent, and accountable transfers, while community awareness activities support nutrition, health, and self reliance 
CN_CRE</narrative>
  </description>
